<article class="env-lemma" id="0F9C">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0F9C">Lemma <span data-tag="0F9C">42.45.2</span>.</a> Let $(S, \delta )$ be as in Situation <a href="/tag/02QL" data-tag="02QL">42.7.1</a>. Let $X$ be locally of finite type over $S$. Let $ 0 \to \mathcal{E}_1 \to \mathcal{E} \to \mathcal{E}_2 \to 0 $ be a short exact sequence of finite locally free $\mathcal{O}_ X$-modules. Then we have the equality </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  ch(\mathcal{E}) = ch(\mathcal{E}_1) + ch(\mathcal{E}_2)  \]
</div>
  <p> More precisely, we have $P_ p(\mathcal{E}) = P_ p(\mathcal{E}_1) + P_ p(\mathcal{E}_2)$ in $A^ p(X)$ where $P_ p$ is as in Example <a href="/tag/0F9B" data-tag="0F9B">42.43.6</a>. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      It suffices to prove the more precise statement. By Section <a href="/tag/02UK" data-tag="02UK">42.43</a> this follows because if $x_{1, i}$, $i = 1, \ldots , r_1$ and $x_{2, i}$, $i = 1, \ldots , r_2$ are the Chern roots of $\mathcal{E}_1$ and $\mathcal{E}_2$, then $x_{1, 1}, \ldots , x_{1, r_1}, x_{2, 1}, \ldots , x_{2, r_2}$ are the Chern roots of $\mathcal{E}$. Hence we get the result from our choice of $P_ p$ in Example <a href="/tag/0F9B" data-tag="0F9B">42.43.6</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>